  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1227 PM CDT WED AUG 26 2026\n\nIt's another hot day across Central Alabama, with high\ntemperatures expected in the low to mid 90s this afternoon. We\nare currently watching an MCS feature over West Tennessee drop\ntoward the south-southeast. This is associated with a shortwave\nat the 500 mb level that is moving around the eastern periphery\nof an upper-level ridge centered over New Mexico and Arizona.\nThis typical northwest flow pattern routinely brings these MCS\nfeatures into Central Alabama, and today looks to be a similar\nsetup. Midday mesoanalysis shows a steep instability gradient\nfrom NE Mississippi to Middle Tennessee, with >2500 and\n>3500 MLCAPE and SBCAPE across NE Mississippi. This is driving\nthe current MCS to propagate more SSE rather than SE or ESE, as\nstorms entering the more stable region of Middle Tennessee will\ndie off while additional activity is developing on the west side\nof the MCS and joining in the SSE movement along this instability\ngradient. A cumulus field developing over the western half of\nCentral Alabama is already promoting the formation of a few\nshowers and storms ahead of the approaching MCS, which we expect\nto reach Marion and Winston counties by around 3 pm. As the\nafternoon moves on, we should see some manner of intensification\nwith the MCS, which will pose a damaging wind threat as it moves\nacross the western part of the area.\n\nActivity should diminish before midnight, but Thursday and Friday\nwill again feature elevated chances for showers and\nthunderstorms. Although no official severe risk area is currently\ndefined, we forecast the greatest potential for thunderstorm\nactivity to be over central and southeastern parts of Central\nAlabama the next two days. The current forecast trends have\nlesser rain chances over the weekend and into the beginning of\nnext week, but still 20-30% chances for now each afternoon from\nSaturday through Tuesday. Most of the rain chances will be\nconcentrated central and southeast, much like Thursday and\nFriday.\n\nHigh temperatures continue at a mostly steady state, with nothing\nout of the ordinary. Highs in the upper 80s to low 90s can be\nexpected Thursday through next Tuesday, with heat indices\nreaching to low 100s at times.\n\n12",
